  "seoDescription": "Compute sequence perplexity from token IDs and precomputed next-token logits using stable log probabilities.",
  "solved": false,
  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Compute sequence Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) and Perplexity from next-token vocabulary logits using numerically stable log-softmax.\n\nPerplexity measures the effective branching factor: a PPL of K indicates the model is as uncertain as choosing uniformly among K words.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\operatorname{NLL} = -\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^T \\log P(x_t), \\quad \\operatorname{PPL} = \\exp(\\operatorname{NLL})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Apply numerically stable log-softmax across vocabulary logits\n2. Extract log probabilities of the target sequence tokens\n3. Compute average sequence cross-entropy and exponentiate for perplexity\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'nll_loss' and 'perplexity' values.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Perplexity Computation\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **Perplexity Computation** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nPerplexity measures the effective branching factor: a PPL of K indicates the model is as uncertain as choosing uniformly among K words. In this problem, the goal is to: compute sequence negative log-likelihood (nll) and perplexity from next-token vocabulary logits using numerically stable log-softmax.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{NLL} = -\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^T \\log P(x_t), \\quad \\operatorname{PPL} = \\exp(\\operatorname{NLL})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Apply numerically stable log-softmax across vocabulary logits\n2. **Phase 2**: Extract log probabilities of the target sequence tokens\n3. **Phase 3**: Compute average sequence cross-entropy and exponentiate for perplexity\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: token_ids = [1, 2, 0], logits = [[0.1, 2.5, 0.3, 0.2], [0.5, 0.1, 3.0, 0.2], [2.0, 0.5, 0.1, 0.4]]\n\nOutput: {\"nll_loss\": 0.2994, \"perplexity\": 1.3491}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated nlp_perplexity_computation under standard test conditions."
